can you give me the most common nixos directory structure ? Assuming i have 3 different machines

Good question — worth being precise here because "module" means two different things depending on whether it's evaluated by the NixOS module system or the home-manager module system. They're technically the same mechanism (the NixOS module system), but they hook into different option trees (config.services.*, config.programs.* at the system level vs config.home.*, config.programs.* at the user level), so people usually keep them physically separated.

A common refinement of the layout:

modules/
├── nixos/          <- system-level modules (evaluated inside nixosConfigurations)
│   ├── common/
│   │   └── default.nix
│   ├── desktop/
│   │   └── gnome.nix
│   └── services/
│       └── nginx.nix
└── home/           <- home-manager modules (evaluated inside home-manager config)
    ├── common/
    │   └── default.nix
    ├── shell.nix
    └── neovim.nix

Example: a NixOS module (system-level)

modules/nixos/services/nginx.nix — this can touch anything under services., networking., users., systemd., etc., because it runs in the system context:
nix

{ config, lib, pkgs, ... }:

with lib;

let
  cfg = config.myModules.nginx;
in {
  options.myModules.nginx = {
    enable = mkEnableOption "nginx web server";
  };

  config = mkIf cfg.enable {
    services.nginx = {
      enable = true;
      recommendedProxySettings = true;
    };

    networking.firewall.allowedTCPPorts = [ 80 443 ];
  };
}

Then in a host's configuration.nix:
nix

{
  imports = [ ../../modules/nixos/services/nginx.nix ];
  myModules.nginx.enable = true;
}

Example: a home-manager module (user-level)

modules/home/neovim.nix — this only touches things under home., programs., xdg., because it runs inside the home-manager context, not the system one:
nix

{ config, lib, pkgs, ... }:

with lib;

let
  cfg = config.myHomeModules.neovim;
in {
  options.myHomeModules.neovim = {
    enable = mkEnableOption "neovim setup";
  };

  config = mkIf cfg.enable {
    programs.neovim = {
      enable = true;
      viAlias = true;
      vimAlias = true;
      extraConfig = ''
        set number
        set relativenumber
      '';
    };

    home.packages = with pkgs; [ ripgrep fd ];
  };
}

Then wired in via a per-user home.nix (or directly in your flake's home-manager block):
nix

{
  imports = [ ../../modules/home/neovim.nix ];
  myHomeModules.neovim.enable = true;
}

Why keep them separate

    A NixOS module can be imported by any nixosConfiguration, including headless servers that never touch home-manager at all.
    A home-manager module gets imported per-user, so if you have two users on the same desktop machine, only one of them might want your neovim.nix module while the other doesn't.
    Mixing them in one folder works technically (nothing stops you), but it becomes confusing fast, because you'll have some files expecting config.services.* and others expecting config.home.*, and nothing in the folder structure signals which is which.

One subtlety: you can define custom options in both (as above, with mkEnableOption), or you can skip the options-pattern entirely and just put raw config directly in the module (services.nginx.enable = true; with no mkIf/option wrapper) — that's simpler for one-off machine-specific tweaks, while the options-pattern is worth it once you're toggling the same module across multiple hosts.

Want me to show how this looks wired up for one of your actual 3 machines, e.g. if one is a headless server that shouldn't pull in any home-manager modules at all?

okay so any reusable configuration goes in modules/ ? And hosts/ and home/ are EXCLUSIVELY to plug that ?

Close, but not quite "exclusively" — there's some nuance worth calling out.

The core idea is right: modules/ holds reusable logic, and hosts//home/ are where you compose modules for a specific machine or user. But in practice hosts/ and home/ files aren't purely wiring — they also hold things that are inherently host-specific and wouldn't make sense as a reusable module because there's only ever one instance of them.
What legitimately lives in hosts/<name>/configuration.nix beyond just imports+toggles:
nix

{ config, pkgs, ... }:

{
  imports = [
    ./hardware-configuration.nix
    ../../modules/nixos/services/nginx.nix
    ../../modules/nixos/common
  ];

  # --- pure wiring: toggling reusable modules ---
  myModules.nginx.enable = true;

  # --- host-specific values that AREN'T reusable modules ---
  networking.hostName = "desktop";           # unique per machine
  networking.hostId = "8425e349";            # needed for zfs, unique per machine
  boot.loader.grub.device = "/dev/sda";      # depends on this machine's disk
  system.stateVersion = "24.05";             # set once per machine, never copied

  # --- one-off machine tweaks not worth turning into a module ---
  boot.kernelParams = [ "amd_pstate=active" ]; # this machine's specific CPU quirk
}

That last category — one-off tweaks — is the gray area. The rule of thumb people generally follow:
If the config...	Put it in...
Applies to more than one host, or might in the future	modules/
Is a fundamental identity of the machine (hostname, disk device, stateVersion)	hosts/<name>/configuration.nix directly
Is a genuine one-off hack for this machine only, unlikely to be reused	hosts/<name>/configuration.nix directly
Auto-generated by the installer	hosts/<name>/hardware-configuration.nix (never touch by hand)

Same logic applies to home/users/you-desktop.nix — it'll have imports pulling in reusable home-modules, plus small things like which monitor layout this specific machine's window manager should use, since that's not shareable across machines either.

So the practical guideline isn't "everything except imports is forbidden here" — it's "if you'd ever copy-paste this into a second host, it belongs in modules/ instead." Anything that's fundamentally singular to that one host stays local to its file.
